    heading: "Compliance basics: Privacy Act 2020, the UEM Act, Fair Trading Act, accessibility and GST",
    paragraphs: [
      "We are engineers, not lawyers or accountants, so none of this is legal or tax advice. It is the practical groundwork we build into New Zealand websites so that when your adviser reviews them, there is less to fix. Please have a qualified professional confirm what applies to your business and approve the final wording of policies.",
      "<strong>Privacy Act 2020 and the information privacy principles.</strong> The Act sets out information privacy principles that cover why and how personal information is collected, how people are told about it, how it is stored and kept secure, and how people can see and correct what is held about them. A key difference from Australia is that there is no small business exemption: a one-person business collecting enquiries through a website form is covered. A newer principle about collecting personal information indirectly, from someone other than the person it is about, was added in recent amendments. We build forms that collect only the fields you need, show a plain-language notice at the point of collection, link to a real privacy statement and send data to storage with proper access controls.",
      "<strong>Cross-border disclosure.</strong> The Act has a specific principle about sending personal information overseas, and because our team is in India, it is worth planning for. Our default is to build and test with dummy data, keep customer data in your own systems and hosting accounts, and access live personal information only when a task genuinely needs it and you have agreed. If your adviser wants particular contract terms about how we handle data, we will sign them.",
      "<strong>Privacy breaches.</strong> Serious privacy breaches must be notified to the Office of the Privacy Commissioner and to affected people. Engineering choices make those breaches less likely: HTTPS everywhere, patched software, two-factor authentication for admin accounts, least-privilege access, tested backups and no personal data sitting in unprotected spreadsheets or inboxes.",
      "<strong>Unsolicited Electronic Messages Act 2007.</strong> Commercial emails and texts need the recipient's consent, must identify the sender accurately, and must include a working unsubscribe that is actioned promptly. The Department of Internal Affairs enforces the Act. We build sign-up forms with unticked consent boxes and clear wording, connect them to email platforms that manage unsubscribes automatically, and record when and how each person opted in.",
      "<strong>Fair Trading Act 1986 and the Consumer Guarantees Act 1993.</strong> The Fair Trading Act prohibits misleading and deceptive conduct and unsubstantiated claims, and its unfair contract terms rules apply to standard form consumer contracts and, since recent changes, to many small trade contracts as well. The Consumer Guarantees Act gives consumers rights that cannot be signed away in ordinary consumer sales. On a website that affects product claims, pricing, reviews, returns pages and terms and conditions. The Commerce Commission enforces these laws.",
      "<strong>Accessibility.</strong> The Human Rights Act 1993 prohibits disability discrimination in providing goods and services, and government agencies follow the New Zealand Government Web Accessibility Standard, which is based on WCAG. We build to WCAG 2.2 level AA as a working target for every site: meaningful headings, keyboard navigation, visible focus, labelled form fields, strong colour contrast, text alternatives and error messages that screen readers announce. If you supply to government or funded organisations with their own requirements, share them and we will build to them.",
      "<strong>GST.</strong> GST is 15 per cent, and businesses must register once their turnover passes the registration threshold. For your website that means GST-inclusive consumer prices, correct invoice information and matching settings in your store and in Xero or your accounting software. For your purchase from us, our starting prices are in US dollars without New Zealand GST; your accountant can confirm how an imported service is treated.",
      "<strong>Sector rules.</strong> Some industries have their own advertising requirements. Health and wellness businesses should be careful with therapeutic claims and follow the Advertising Standards Authority's codes. Licensed building practitioners, real estate agents and financial advice providers have registration details that belong on their sites. Alcohol sellers need age verification and must follow licensing conditions. We build the structure; you supply the regulated wording.",
